---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SectionIntro from '../components/SectionIntro.astro';
import SourceList from '../components/SourceList.astro';
import CalloutPanel from '../components/CalloutPanel.astro';
import { sources } from '../data/sources';

const pageTitle = 'How America became the outlier';
const pageDescription =
  'Routine infant circumcision entered American medicine as a Victorian cure for masturbation. The rest of the English-speaking world walked it back; the US did not. A sourced history.';
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} pageKey="history">
  <section class="section section--tight history-head">
    <div class="container container--reading">
      <h1>How America became the outlier</h1>
      <p class="history-head__lede">
        The United States is the only country in the world that circumcises a
        large share of its newborn boys for non-religious, non-medical
        reasons. It did not arrive there by discovering something the rest of
        the world missed. It arrived there by not letting go of something the
        rest of the world abandoned. Here is the sourced history of how that
        happened.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container container--reading history-prose">
      <SectionIntro
        eyebrow="1870s–1890s"
        heading="A Victorian cure for masturbation"
      />
      <p>
        Routine circumcision entered English-speaking medicine in the late
        1800s, and the problem it was meant to solve was not infection. It was
        masturbation, then believed to cause everything from epilepsy to
        insanity. In 1870 the prominent American surgeon Lewis Sayre reported
        curing a boy's partial paralysis by attending to his foreskin, and the
        idea spread that the prepuce was a source of nervous "reflex" disease.
      </p>
      <p>
        The most-quoted figure of the era is Dr. John Harvey Kellogg — the
        cereal magnate and physician — who in <em>Plain Facts for Old and
        Young</em> recommended circumcision as a remedy for the habit in boys,
        and was explicit about why the pain was a feature rather than a
        drawback:
      </p>
      <blockquote class="history-quote">
        "The operation should be performed by a surgeon without administering
        an anesthetic, as the brief pain attending the operation will have a
        salutary effect upon the mind, especially if it be connected with the
        idea of punishment."
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        That is the origin. The practice was not built on evidence that it
        made children healthier; it was built on a theory of the body that
        medicine has since discarded entirely. Every modern health rationale —
        UTIs, HIV, cancer — was attached later, to a procedure that was
        already looking for a justification.
      </p>
      <SourceList
        sources={[sources.kellogg1888, sources.sayre1870, sources.gollaher1994]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container container--reading history-prose">
      <SectionIntro
        eyebrow="1949 onward"
        heading="How the rest of the English-speaking world walked it back"
      />
      <p>
        In 1949 the British physician Douglas Gairdner published "The fate of
        the foreskin" in the <em>BMJ</em>, laying out that the foreskin is
        normal developing tissue, that infant circumcision carried real risks,
        and that the deaths it caused were not worth the theoretical benefits.
        Britain's new National Health Service never covered routine
        circumcision, and the paper is widely credited with hastening a
        decline that was already under way. British circumcision fell from
        roughly a third of boys in the 1930s to a few percent by the 1980s.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rest of the Commonwealth followed the same path. The Canadian
        Paediatric Society has advised against routine newborn circumcision
        since 1975. The Royal Australasian College of Physicians concluded the
        evidence does not warrant it, and Australian public hospitals stopped
        performing it. In New Zealand, near-universal in the WWII era, routine
        circumcision collapsed to under 1% of boys by the late 1970s.
      </p>
      <p>
        By the end of the twentieth century every comparable country had
        reached the same conclusion: healthy foreskins should be left alone.
        Every country except one.
      </p>
      <SourceList
        sources={[
          sources.gairdner1949,
          sources.rickwood2000,
          sources.cps2015,
          sources.racpStatement,
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container container--reading history-prose">
      <SectionIntro
        eyebrow="The American exception"
        heading="Why the United States kept going"
      />
      <p>
        America did not stay the outlier because its doctors saw evidence
        others missed. Three ordinary forces kept the practice alive after its
        original rationale collapsed.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Money.</strong> For decades circumcision was a routine,
        reimbursed line item. When that changed, behavior changed with it:
        researchers found newborn circumcision rates were markedly lower in
        states whose Medicaid programs stopped covering it, and a study of ten
        states that dropped coverage found circumcision among affected
        newborns fell by an average of about 21 percentage points afterward.
        A practice that tracks insurance billing that closely is not being
        driven by medical necessity.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Habit.</strong> Once a generation of doctors is trained to do
        something and a generation of parents expects it, the default
        perpetuates itself. The most common reason given for circumcising a
        son has long been that his father was circumcised — a reason that is
        cultural, not clinical.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The "he should match" prophecy.</strong> Parents were told
        their sons would feel out of place if left intact. That prediction is
        now failing on its own terms: as fewer boys are circumcised each year,
        the intact boy is increasingly the one who matches his peers.
      </p>
      <SourceList
        sources={[
          sources.leibowitz2009,
          sources.medicaidDecline2021,
          sources.aapTechReport,
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container container--reading history-prose">
      <SectionIntro
        eyebrow="A footnote that isn't small"
        heading="For most of this history, there was no pain relief"
      />
      <p>
        It is worth pausing on one detail. For most of the twentieth century,
        American infant circumcision was performed with no anesthesia at all —
        partly on the long-discredited assumption that newborns did not really
        feel or remember pain. That assumption was tested directly. In a 1997
        randomized trial in <em>JAMA</em>, the babies circumcised without
        anesthetic showed such sustained distress — racing heart rates,
        high-pitched crying throughout, and two infants who became ill
        afterward with choking and a pause in breathing — that the researchers
        concluded an anesthetic should be given for every newborn
        circumcision.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pain control has improved since, but it is still incomplete, and the
        history matters: a procedure whose Victorian selling point was that the
        pain taught a lesson was, a century later, still being done to infants
        who were given nothing for it.
      </p>
      <SourceList sources={[sources.lander1997, sources.taddio1997]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container container--reading history-prose">
      <SectionIntro
        eyebrow="Where it stands now"
        heading="The exception is fading"
      />
      <p>
        The American consensus is quietly dissolving. In-hospital newborn
        circumcision fell from 64.5% in 1979 to 58.3% in 2010, and a 2025
        analysis in <em>JAMA Pediatrics</em> put it near 49% by 2022 — meaning
        about half of recent American boys are now left intact. The regional
        split is stark: roughly 69% in the Midwest against about 20% in the
        West. A generation from now, today's parents may find the practice as
        difficult to explain as their grandparents' reasons already sound.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of this history is an argument by itself — a practice can be old
        and wrong, or old and right. But it answers the question people rarely
        ask: not "is circumcision worth it?" so much as "why do we do this at
        all?" The honest answer is that we started for reasons no one would
        defend today, and much of the world has already stopped.
      </p>
      <SourceList sources={[sources.cdcRates, sources.jamaPeds2025]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CalloutPanel
    heading="What the evidence says today"
    body="History explains how we got here. The evidence page covers where the medical case actually stands now — the organization positions, the anatomy, the health claims, and the risks."
    primaryLabel="Read the evidence"
    primaryHref="/evidence"
    secondaryLabel="Common claims, answered"
    secondaryHref="/faq"
  />
</Layout>

<style>
  .history-head {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-5);
  }

  .history-head h1 {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .history-head__lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-body);
  }

  .history-prose p {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .history-quote {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
    padding: var(--space-5) var(--space-6);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-amber);
    background-color: rgba(156, 107, 47, 0.06);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.7;
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius) var(--radius) 0;
  }
</style>
